[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 1: White Attacks With 10.g4 and 12.h5  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/2bccec30-270b-4e06-8e6b-fd14ccd8d93a/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to our course on the Sicilian Dragon. The Dragon is one of the most popular variations of the Sicilian where we play on the dark squares in the center, but fianchetto the dark-squared bishop early. The main line is famous and long, and players usually castle on opposite sides, making the Dragon known for sharp, tactical and aggressive battles!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. This move prepares d4 anyway, which is fine as we want to trade when that pawn arrives in the center. We will create the pawn chain on the dark squares, keeping our structure and our central presence solid. This blocks the dark-squared bishop but we will activate it to g7 in the Dragon.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. Allowing them to keep both pawns there would be giving them too much power. So we will capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. Time to start piece development. The knight on f6 attacks the central e4-pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends by bringing the knight to c3. This is the famous starting position in the Sicilian. We'll fianchetto. The pawn structure often prompts White to castle long and attack our king. On the other hand, once White does castle long, our bishop on g7 becomes one of the most important pieces.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White could choose to reply with a number of different moves, but we will stick to the most common ones and the main line in Chapter 1. Be3 is played 35% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games! The idea behind Be3 is to play Qd2 in the future and try to trade off the dark-squared bishops. We will fianchetto either way, threatening ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {Which is why White usually plays f3. We'll castle kingside, and activate the queenside pieces next.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White goes ahead with their plan and creates the bishop and queen battery 80% of the time. This also prepares queenside castling. After ...Nc6, our intention is not to trade on d4 but to slowly improve our position and wait for White to commit to castling.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {And they do. The main line for Black is actually 9...d5 here, striking in the center, but I recommend developing with ...Bd7. It could be considered a bit risky because we take some time to get all the pieces out. However, it's also an option that gives us a straightforward attacking plan and a brilliant trap many players fall into.} 9...Bd7 10.g4 {Meanwhile, White starts the kingside operations. Here, g4 is played over 40% of the time. White's idea is simple: h4 and h5. To counter that, we are going to play on the queenside. Next, a good plan would be to try and get the knight to c4 to target White's key attackers.} 10...Rc8 11.h4 {White continues pushing, unbothered. The pawn is coming to h5, but we can still afford to play in the center. And we'll place the knight on e5. It pressures the f3-square from there and wants to jump to c4. It seems like White is much quicker with their attack...} 11...Ne5 12.h5 {Especially when they play h5, already chipping away at our structure, which is what Study 1 is about. It's played two thirds of the time. But we have ...Qa5! From the a5-square, the queen is pressuring both the knight on c3 and the pawn on a2.} 12...Qa5 13.hxg6 {White could disregard our expansion completely and just capture on g6. The correct recapture is with the f-pawn. That allows our f8-rook to join the defense on f7 if needed. And if White's queen leaves the post on d2, we can actually infiltrate with the exchange sacrifice on c3.} 13...fxg6 14.Bh6 {White might sense blood in the water and go for the trade now. But this is incorrect. We'll take.} 14...Bxh6 15.Qxh6 {So that the queen arrives on h7. Our knight is guarding the h7-square for now, and there is no danger. Which means we can sacrifice on c3!} 15...Rxc3 16.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 16...Qxc3 {And now our queen comes in, with the threat of ...Qa1+. The position is still equal according to the computer but one little slip might decide the game in an unstable position like this. White's best move is probably Kb1 after which we can go for ...b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 1: White Attacks With 10.g4 and 12.h5  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/2bccec30-270b-4e06-8e6b-fd14ccd8d93a/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to our course on the Sicilian Dragon. The Dragon is one of the most popular variations of the Sicilian where we play on the dark squares in the center, but fianchetto the dark-squared bishop early. The main line is famous and long, and players usually castle on opposite sides, making the Dragon known for sharp, tactical and aggressive battles!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. This move prepares d4 anyway, which is fine as we want to trade when that pawn arrives in the center. We will create the pawn chain on the dark squares, keeping our structure and our central presence solid. This blocks the dark-squared bishop but we will activate it to g7 in the Dragon.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. Allowing them to keep both pawns there would be giving them too much power. So we will capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. Time to start piece development. The knight on f6 attacks the central e4-pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends by bringing the knight to c3. This is the famous starting position in the Sicilian. We'll fianchetto. The pawn structure often prompts White to castle long and attack our king. On the other hand, once White does castle long, our bishop on g7 becomes one of the most important pieces.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White could choose to reply with a number of different moves, but we will stick to the most common ones and the main line in Chapter 1. Be3 is played 35% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games! The idea behind Be3 is to play Qd2 in the future and try to trade off the dark-squared bishops. We will fianchetto either way, threatening ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {Which is why White usually plays f3. We'll castle kingside, and activate the queenside pieces next.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White goes ahead with their plan and creates the bishop and queen battery 80% of the time. This also prepares queenside castling. After ...Nc6, our intention is not to trade on d4 but to slowly improve our position and wait for White to commit to castling.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {And they do. The main line for Black is actually 9...d5 here, striking in the center, but I recommend developing with ...Bd7. It could be considered a bit risky because we take some time to get all the pieces out. However, it's also an option that gives us a straightforward attacking plan and a brilliant trap many players fall into.} 9...Bd7 10.g4 {Meanwhile, White starts the kingside operations. Here, g4 is played over 40% of the time. White's idea is simple: h4 and h5. To counter that, we are going to play on the queenside. Next, a good plan would be to try and get the knight to c4 to target White's key attackers.} 10...Rc8 11.h4 {White continues pushing, unbothered. The pawn is coming to h5, but we can still afford to play in the center. And we'll place the knight on e5. It pressures the f3-square from there and wants to jump to c4. It seems like White is much quicker with their attack...} 11...Ne5 12.h5 {Especially when they play h5, already chipping away at our structure, which is what Study 1 is about. It's played two thirds of the time. But we have ...Qa5! From the a5-square, the queen is pressuring both the knight on c3 and the pawn on a2.} 12...Qa5 13.Kb1 {Kb1 is a very natural move to slide the king away from the rook on c8 as well as defend the a2-pawn. However, this allows us to spring our powerful trap, and that's to take on f3 with the knight! The idea is to unleash the powerful bishop on g7 and remove the guard of e4 and g4. For the price of the knight, White will find it extremely hard to defend all the targets, as you will see.} 13...Nxf3 14.Nxf3 {But when White captures on f3, that's not all. We can implement a standard and thematic exchange sacrifice here! Now sacrificing a whole rook, we want the c3-pawn to eventually be a target for the knight. You are about to see how well this position works for us tactically.} 14...Rxc3 15.Qxc3 {White captures with the queen. Since they're up material, they want to trade as much as possible. Which is fine. We counted on it.} 15...Qxc3 16.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures. Now that we set everything up the way we wanted, it's time to gobble up the pawns.} 16...Bxg4 {First, we'll capture on g4, pinning the knight and threatening to take with a fork. If White plays Be2, we can capture on e4 and threaten multiple forks. If White tries to go for Bd4 at some point, ...e5 is a good move to keep the momentum. We'll have a lot of pawns and plenty of compensation for the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 1: White Attacks With 10.g4 and 12.h5  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/2bccec30-270b-4e06-8e6b-fd14ccd8d93a/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to our course on the Sicilian Dragon. The Dragon is one of the most popular variations of the Sicilian where we play on the dark squares in the center, but fianchetto the dark-squared bishop early. The main line is famous and long, and players usually castle on opposite sides, making the Dragon known for sharp, tactical and aggressive battles!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. This move prepares d4 anyway, which is fine as we want to trade when that pawn arrives in the center. We will create the pawn chain on the dark squares, keeping our structure and our central presence solid. This blocks the dark-squared bishop but we will activate it to g7 in the Dragon.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. Allowing them to keep both pawns there would be giving them too much power. So we will capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. Time to start piece development. The knight on f6 attacks the central e4-pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends by bringing the knight to c3. This is the famous starting position in the Sicilian. We'll fianchetto. The pawn structure often prompts White to castle long and attack our king. On the other hand, once White does castle long, our bishop on g7 becomes one of the most important pieces.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White could choose to reply with a number of different moves, but we will stick to the most common ones and the main line in Chapter 1. Be3 is played 35% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games! The idea behind Be3 is to play Qd2 in the future and try to trade off the dark-squared bishops. We will fianchetto either way, threatening ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {Which is why White usually plays f3. We'll castle kingside, and activate the queenside pieces next.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White goes ahead with their plan and creates the bishop and queen battery 80% of the time. This also prepares queenside castling. After ...Nc6, our intention is not to trade on d4 but to slowly improve our position and wait for White to commit to castling.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {And they do. The main line for Black is actually 9...d5 here, striking in the center, but I recommend developing with ...Bd7. It could be considered a bit risky because we take some time to get all the pieces out. However, it's also an option that gives us a straightforward attacking plan and a brilliant trap many players fall into.} 9...Bd7 10.g4 {Meanwhile, White starts the kingside operations. Here, g4 is played over 40% of the time. White's idea is simple: h4 and h5. To counter that, we are going to play on the queenside. Next, a good plan would be to try and get the knight to c4 to target White's key attackers.} 10...Rc8 11.g5 {Pushing the g-pawn all the way is the wrong approach for White.} 11...Nh5 {After ...Nh5, their attack is blocked. This is not the way to play for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 2: Move 12 Alternatives after 10.g4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/f9222d22-a0b0-4f30-8ab5-a18d4658a1ad/lines"]

1.e4 {Study 2 will deal with some alternatives for White on move 12, such as 12.Be2 and 12.Kb1. White moving the bishop to e2 might suggest they know about our trap from Study 1 and want to stop it. On the other hand, 12.Kb1 often leads to sharp lines where we are the ones who can come out on top!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight and preparing d4. We'll strengthen our pawn chain.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. It's best to capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn in the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The Dragon!} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White goes for the main Be3. Keep in mind that ...Ng4 here loses to Bb5+. So we will fianchetto first.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {White stops ...Ng4 now. And we'll castle, having developed the kingside.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White lines up the queen with the bishop and prepares castling. And we'll activate the knight to the best square.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {White finally castles. And we will go with our usual plan of ...Bd7. The rook is coming to the c-file and the knight is coming to the center.} 9...Bd7 10.g4 {The most standard is White beginning to advance their pawns in this position. Which we don't have to be afraid of yet.} 10...Rc8 11.h4 {White pushes the h-pawn as well. While our knight begins its journey in the center. The last study showed the aggressive 12.h5.} 11...Ne5 12.Kb1 {White might play the prophylactic Kb1 on move 12, which is done about 10% of the time. This could become a tricky line. We'll play ...Qa5 to attack the knight and threaten the sacrifice as in the main line. But White played Kb1 for a reason.} 12...Qa5 13.Nd5 {Now that the a2-pawn is defended, they want to move the knight to the center and offer a queen trade. This is usually a trick we need to watch out for when White plays Kb1, but here White is the one getting tricked! We'll take. What is this trick about?} 13...Qxd2 14.Nxe7+ {Well, White's idea is that they have an intermezzo on e7. But they would have to be extremely accurate not to lose the game here, which I doubt many players will. We'll evade the check, and now White has to recapture the queen first.} 14...Kh8 15.Rxd2 {Which doesn't give them time to take our rook on c8. We've lost a pawn so far, but that's not all. The knight is now under attack and there are only two moves that don't lose the game for White on the spot, which are g5 and h5. But even if White knows the theory here, they are also tricky and give us some advantage.} 15...Rce8 16.Nd5 {If they play the natural Nd5 instead, it's over. We'll take the knight.} 16...Nxd5 17.exd5 {The pawn has to recapture, and the crucial aspect of the position is the undefended bishop on e3. There's a tactic here. We can capture on f3 and we are infiltrating.} 17...Nxf3 18.Nxf3 {So they have to take on f3.} 18...Rxe3 {But now the bishop falls and the knight on f3 is under attack, as well as the pawn on g4. We will be up a pawn with a bishop pair, controlling the only open file in the position. Black is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 2: Move 12 Alternatives after 10.g4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/f9222d22-a0b0-4f30-8ab5-a18d4658a1ad/lines"]

1.e4 {Study 2 will deal with some alternatives for White on move 12, such as 12.Be2 and 12.Kb1. White moving the bishop to e2 might suggest they know about our trap from Study 1 and want to stop it. On the other hand, 12.Kb1 often leads to sharp lines where we are the ones who can come out on top!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight and preparing d4. We'll strengthen our pawn chain.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. It's best to capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll attack the pawn in the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The Dragon!} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White goes for the main Be3. Keep in mind that ...Ng4 here loses to Bb5+. So we will fianchetto first.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {White stops ...Ng4 now. And we'll castle, having developed the kingside.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White lines up the queen with the bishop and prepares castling. And we'll activate the knight to the best square.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {White finally castles. And we will go with our usual plan of ...Bd7. The rook is coming to the c-file and the knight is coming to the center.} 9...Bd7 10.g4 {The most standard is White beginning to advance their pawns in this position. Which we don't have to be afraid of yet.} 10...Rc8 11.h4 {White pushes the h-pawn as well. While our knight begins its journey in the center. The last study showed the aggressive 12.h5.} 11...Ne5 12.Be2 {In order to avoid our ...Nxf3 trap in the future, White has to know this key idea, placing the bishop on e2. This move is played 17% of the time. It does solve that problem for White but gives us the momentum to play aggressive moves. One of them is placing the queen on a5. White could go on the defensive here.} 12...Qa5 13.Kb1 {This isn't winning for us or anything, but the Dragon is so sharp that often the side that's allowed to attack first wins. And we will definitely put some pressure on White's position now by sacrificing the rook on c3! Even at the price of trading queens, giving up the rook for the knight on c3 allows us to exploit White's key weaknesses down the line.} 13...Rxc3 14.Qxc3 {The queen takes. Trading queens is fine.} 14...Qxc3 15.bxc3 {Now White has some serious targets around the king. Which we will begin to attack immediately. White's weak pawns and the exposed king are what provides compensation for the sacrifice and the queen trade.} 15...Rc8 16.Kb2 {The king can defend, but we're not done applying pressure.} 16...Be8 {A nice move to remember is ...Be8! We want to free the d7-square for the knight which can become one of our main attacking pieces. It's coming to b6, and then to c4 or a4. White will certainly have to react to our moves for a while without thinking about attacking themselves at all.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 3: White Plays 10.h4 and Bh6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/690b60c3-64ca-4f5a-8152-7b964ee3e492/lines"]

1.e4 {Let's focus on White's deviations from the main line with 10.g4. In particular, these include White attacking with the h-pawn first or going for the exchange on h6 immediately. Both of these moves could be considered suboptimal as we have solutions for each of them.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll reinforce the center.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances with d4. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. Time to develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the Dragon Sicilian.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White activates the dark-squared bishop. We'll fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {Stopping ...Ng4. Time to castle.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White prepares castling as well. And we'll develop the knight to the active c6-square.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {Opposite side castling usually signifies a fierce battle to come. Let's develop the bishop with the standard ideas and await White's next move. One of the ideas is Kb1, which transposes. Another option is Bc4 but placing the bishop on the c-file plays into our pieces.} 9...Bd7 10.h4 {White starts with h4 first just over 15% of the time. We can play ...Rc8 here and the positions will likely transpose to lines we've already covered after g4. However, there is an additional option. Since White hasn't played g4, we can try to block the attack! White shouldn't go crazy here with g4 and h5 attempts.} 10...h5 11.Be2 {Instead, they can activate the bishop and perhaps prepare for g4 to come in the future. But this is already much slower than g4 right away.} 11...Rc8 {We'll continue with our standard ideas. The knight is coming to the center and our queen will join the queenside. I would say this is more favorable for us than 10.g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line 9...Bd7 / Study 3: White Plays 10.h4 and Bh6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/82edc038-ba22-4006-ab3e-0995dae4411c/studies/690b60c3-64ca-4f5a-8152-7b964ee3e492/lines"]

1.e4 {Let's focus on White's deviations from the main line with 10.g4. In particular, these include White attacking with the h-pawn first or going for the exchange on h6 immediately. Both of these moves could be considered suboptimal as we have solutions for each of them.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Activating the knight. We'll reinforce the center.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances with d4. Let's take.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. Time to develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. Let's go for the Dragon Sicilian.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White activates the dark-squared bishop. We'll fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {Stopping ...Ng4. Time to castle.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {White prepares castling as well. And we'll develop the knight to the active c6-square.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {Opposite side castling usually signifies a fierce battle to come. Let's develop the bishop with the standard ideas and await White's next move. One of the ideas is Kb1, which transposes. Another option is Bc4 but placing the bishop on the c-file plays into our pieces.} 9...Bd7 10.Bh6 {When going through the main line, I mentioned that White's general idea with Be3 and Qd2 is to try and trade off the bishops on h6 at some point. In this position, it's a mistake, though. It's too early and fails due a tactical combination from our side. We can capture on d4 and force White's hand!} 10...Nxd4 11.Bxg7 {White has to take on g7 first. But now we have an intermezzo. We can capture on f3 and attack the queen!} 11...Nxf3 12.gxf3 {White has to recapture.} 12...Kxg7 {And after we take, our kingside is intact and we've won a clean pawn. White doesn't have an attack and we'll go for the usual ...Rc8 and ...Qa5. The queen could even go to b6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Yugoslav Attack / Study 1: White Plays 8.Bc4  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/4107b8a9-7ef0-4a39-aad3-49e557373521/studies/cdcac7d9-a15d-47cb-b5b8-15cf917f724b/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Qd2 {Instead of giving us another tempo, White could continue in the most thematic way with Qd2, preparing castling on the queenside. We'll continue in a thematic way as well and activate the bishop. You already know our ideas.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O-O {In fact, when White castles... Our ideas are even more potent because White has to spend another move retreating with the bishop.} 10...Rc8 11.Bb3 {They retreat to b3. Now, we could go for the standard ...Ne5 and ...Nc4 idea, but I propose something else. Let's capture on d4, relieve some tension and open up the rook. Instead of going the slower and positional route, let's attack on the queenside as soon as possible.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bxd4 {White recaptures. And now we can push ...b5 with the idea of a pawn storm on the queenside. Our rook is already on the perfect square and our queen is waiting to join. In fact, White has scored poorly in practice from this position because our attack is much quicker, simpler and direct.} 12...b5 13.h4 {They can try to counterattack on the kingside. The position is still equal according to the computer, but remember that whoever strikes first might just win. In that spirit, we'll continue pushing. White can either choose to react to our attack here or continue attempting to attack themselves.} 13...a5 14.a3 {A more reasonable defensive move than a4 is definitely a3. White doesn't commit to tactical sequences but rather takes away the b4-square. Or do they? We will still play ...b4!} 14...b4 15.axb4 {White captures. But we keep a pawn on b4. If White isn't careful now and plays something like Ne2, there could be ...Qa5 ideas in the near future.} 15...axb4 16.Nd5 {They have to jump to either d5 or a2. And d5 is the more active square to try and trade off some pieces. We'll capture on d5, opening up our bishop. White has a choice between capturing the bishop on g7 or the knight on d5. One of the captures loses.} 16...Nxd5 17.Bxg7 {Capturing on g7 keeps the position in the balance. If we go for ...Qa5, White has the amazing Qh6. Let's just recapture instead.} 17...Kxg7 18.exd5 {Now White trades in the center. We can bring the queen to a5, but it is not as dangerous anymore.} 18...Qa5 19.Qd4+ {Because White has a check on d4. We'll slide back to g8.} 19...Kg8 20.h5 {White might think they're the one attacking after h5. But it's not that simple.} 20...Bf5 {We still have the light-squared bishop, and we're threatening to capture on c2 and then push ...b3. This combination could be devastating for White if they allow it.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Yugoslav Attack / Study 1: White Plays 8.Bc4  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/4107b8a9-7ef0-4a39-aad3-49e557373521/studies/cdcac7d9-a15d-47cb-b5b8-15cf917f724b/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Qd2 {Instead of giving us another tempo, White could continue in the most thematic way with Qd2, preparing castling on the queenside. We'll continue in a thematic way as well and activate the bishop. You already know our ideas.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O-O {In fact, when White castles... Our ideas are even more potent because White has to spend another move retreating with the bishop.} 10...Rc8 11.Bb3 {They retreat to b3. Now, we could go for the standard ...Ne5 and ...Nc4 idea, but I propose something else. Let's capture on d4, relieve some tension and open up the rook. Instead of going the slower and positional route, let's attack on the queenside as soon as possible.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bxd4 {White recaptures. And now we can push ...b5 with the idea of a pawn storm on the queenside. Our rook is already on the perfect square and our queen is waiting to join. In fact, White has scored poorly in practice from this position because our attack is much quicker, simpler and direct.} 12...b5 13.h4 {They can try to counterattack on the kingside. The position is still equal according to the computer, but remember that whoever strikes first might just win. In that spirit, we'll continue pushing. White can either choose to react to our attack here or continue attempting to attack themselves.} 13...a5 14.a3 {A more reasonable defensive move than a4 is definitely a3. White doesn't commit to tactical sequences but rather takes away the b4-square. Or do they? We will still play ...b4!} 14...b4 15.axb4 {White captures. But we keep a pawn on b4. If White isn't careful now and plays something like Ne2, there could be ...Qa5 ideas in the near future.} 15...axb4 16.Nd5 {They have to jump to either d5 or a2. And d5 is the more active square to try and trade off some pieces. We'll capture on d5, opening up our bishop. White has a choice between capturing the bishop on g7 or the knight on d5. One of the captures loses.} 16...Nxd5 17.Bxd5 {Capturing on d5 loses the game on the spot. Due to an idea I already mentioned, ...Qa5. We are threatening checkmate in one and it's actually not easy to stop it.} 17...Qa5 18.Kb1 {Sliding the king to b1 doesn't work. Do you see a powerful sacrifice? We need a rook on a8. But one rook is enough!} 18...Ra8 19.Bxa8 {White captures. And we'll recapture on a8. The queen is coming.} 19...Rxa8 20.b3 {The position was winning for us anyway, but b3 leads to a checkmate in two moves. It starts with a queen sacrifice.} 20...Qa1+ 21.Bxa1 {White has to capture.} 21...Rxa1# {Checkmate.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Yugoslav Attack / Study 1: White Plays 8.Bc4  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/4107b8a9-7ef0-4a39-aad3-49e557373521/studies/cdcac7d9-a15d-47cb-b5b8-15cf917f724b/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Qd2 {Instead of giving us another tempo, White could continue in the most thematic way with Qd2, preparing castling on the queenside. We'll continue in a thematic way as well and activate the bishop. You already know our ideas.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O-O {In fact, when White castles... Our ideas are even more potent because White has to spend another move retreating with the bishop.} 10...Rc8 11.Bb3 {They retreat to b3. Now, we could go for the standard ...Ne5 and ...Nc4 idea, but I propose something else. Let's capture on d4, relieve some tension and open up the rook. Instead of going the slower and positional route, let's attack on the queenside as soon as possible.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bxd4 {White recaptures. And now we can push ...b5 with the idea of a pawn storm on the queenside. Our rook is already on the perfect square and our queen is waiting to join. In fact, White has scored poorly in practice from this position because our attack is much quicker, simpler and direct.} 12...b5 13.h4 {They can try to counterattack on the kingside. The position is still equal according to the computer, but remember that whoever strikes first might just win. In that spirit, we'll continue pushing. White can either choose to react to our attack here or continue attempting to attack themselves.} 13...a5 14.h5 {White declares war with h5. Let's see who is faster. We'll attack the bishop.} 14...a4 15.Bd5 {White could escape to d5 here, but that's a mistake when they don't take on f6 first. Because we have this cheeky ...e5 idea.} 15...e5 16.Be3 {This is important because it avoids any trades, further restricts White's space and keeps the momentum going. Now we can attack the knight as well.} 16...b4 17.Ne2 {White retreats to e2. Another reason behind Bxf6 being necessary before Bd5 is that we can use our knight to trade on d5 now. And White has to recapture with the queen to try and keep hold of the game. However, even that looks extremely weak after ...Qc7.} 17...Nxd5 18.exd5 {But if White captures with the pawn, it's over.} 18...Bf5 {We can target the c2-pawn with and there's no Nd4 for White to defend with thanks to our ...e5 idea. There are no defensive moves other than that. A move like c3 will prove not to work eventually. We're winning.} *

