1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Qd2 {Instead of giving us another tempo, White could continue in the most thematic way with Qd2, preparing castling on the queenside. We'll continue in a thematic way as well and activate the bishop. You already know our ideas.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O-O {In fact, when White castles... Our ideas are even more potent because White has to spend another move retreating with the bishop.} 10...Rc8 11.Bb3 {They retreat to b3. Now, we could go for the standard ...Ne5 and ...Nc4 idea, but I propose something else. Let's capture on d4, relieve some tension and open up the rook. Instead of going the slower and positional route, let's attack on the queenside as soon as possible.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bxd4 {White recaptures. And now we can push ...b5 with the idea of a pawn storm on the queenside. Our rook is already on the perfect square and our queen is waiting to join. In fact, White has scored poorly in practice from this position because our attack is much quicker, simpler and direct.} 12...b5 13.h4 {They can try to counterattack on the kingside. The position is still equal according to the computer, but remember that whoever strikes first might just win. In that spirit, we'll continue pushing. White can either choose to react to our attack here or continue attempting to attack themselves.} 13...a5 14.h5 {White declares war with h5. Let's see who is faster. We'll attack the bishop.} 14...a4 15.Bxf6 {Capturing on f6 is not a blunder, but giving up the bishop like this isn't the best from a positional standpoint. Still, it's probably the best move according to the computer because there aren't many good alternatives. We'll gladly take and look to push ...f5 at some point. This hangs the d6-pawn, but White's bishop is still under attack.} 15...exf6 16.Bd5 {So they slide to d5. They won't be capturing our d6-pawn any time soon. Meanwhile, we can continue harassing them with ...b4.} 16...b4 17.Ne2 {The knight retreats to e2, and now our pawns are pretty far advanced. But let's take a move to play something else first. We need to open up our bishop.} 17...f5 18.hxg6 {White captures on g6.} 18...hxg6 {We'll recapture on g6 and we should be getting to White's king first with the a- and b-pawns. White isn't that quick with the attack. Our rook and bishop on g7 are already pressuring the key weak spots around the king and our queen will be joining them soon.} *

