1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Qd2 {Instead of giving us another tempo, White could continue in the most thematic way with Qd2, preparing castling on the queenside. We'll continue in a thematic way as well and activate the bishop. You already know our ideas.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O-O {In fact, when White castles... Our ideas are even more potent because White has to spend another move retreating with the bishop.} 10...Rc8 11.Bb3 {They retreat to b3. Now, we could go for the standard ...Ne5 and ...Nc4 idea, but I propose something else. Let's capture on d4, relieve some tension and open up the rook. Instead of going the slower and positional route, let's attack on the queenside as soon as possible.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bxd4 {White recaptures. And now we can push ...b5 with the idea of a pawn storm on the queenside. Our rook is already on the perfect square and our queen is waiting to join. In fact, White has scored poorly in practice from this position because our attack is much quicker, simpler and direct.} 12...b5 13.h4 {They can try to counterattack on the kingside. The position is still equal according to the computer, but remember that whoever strikes first might just win. In that spirit, we'll continue pushing. White can either choose to react to our attack here or continue attempting to attack themselves.} 13...a5 14.a4 {The second most popular move in the position is a4, and it loses the game! We've got a forcing combination. Let's take on a4.} 14...bxa4 15.Nxa4 {White recaptures with the knight first. We'll trade again.} 15...Bxa4 16.Bxa4 {Why did we make these trades? Because we've got a fork on c4!} 16...Rc4 17.Bb3 {But what's our idea? White can simply retreat to b3.} 17...Nxe4 {We can capture on e4! White's queen is under attack so they don't have time to capture the rook on c4. And if they capture the knight on e4, we will take the bishop on d4! This is extremely strong for us because we can bring the rook back to b4 at some point and play ...a4, with the bishop on g7 now being a monster.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Yugoslav Attack / Study 1: White Plays 8.Bc4  / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/4107b8a9-7ef0-4a39-aad3-49e557373521/studies/cdcac7d9-a15d-47cb-b5b8-15cf917f724b/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O {White could castle short here, trying to avoid complications on opposite sides of the board. But there are still some tactical motifs we can take advantage of in this position. Let's activate the queen to b6 and attack the b2-pawn. This won't be the queen's only role, however.} 9...Qb6 10.Bb3 {Once White defends, we have a tactic. The queen is also placed on the critical dark-squared diagonal. We can play ...Ng4 with a discovered attack on the knight on d4! White's options are limited here because we are also attacking the bishop on e3.} 10...Ng4 11.fxg4 {So they have to accept the sacrifice and ruin their pawn structure. Now the g-pawns are doubled and the e-pawn is isolated. This means an endgame will probably be favorable for us in the long run. So let's trade everything off.} 11...Bxd4 12.Bxd4 {White captures. And we'll force a trade of queens, too. White is not going to be able to defend as easily without the queens on the board.} 12...Qxd4+ 13.Qxd4 {White trades. We'll recapture.} 13...Nxd4 14.Nd5 {And now the knight comes in to attack the e7-pawn. We don't have to trade on b3 as well and open up White's rook.} 14...Nc6 {Let's fall back and protect the pawn instead. Our bishop is coming to e6 next and we can play ...Kg7 to improve the king for the endgame and unpin the f7-pawn. We will play against White's weak pawns in the long run.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Yugoslav Attack / Study 1: White Plays 8.Bc4  / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/4107b8a9-7ef0-4a39-aad3-49e557373521/studies/cdcac7d9-a15d-47cb-b5b8-15cf917f724b/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Bb3 {White could spend another tempo bringing the bishop back to b3. Sure, they've established it as an additional piece participating in the game, but gave us two free moves to maneuver. We'll activate the bishop, as per usual. The rook is coming to c8.} 9...Bd7 10.Qd2 {White going for Qd2 is a little bit different. Our knight and bishop are already out. We can trade on d4.} 10...Nxd4 11.Bxd4 {The idea is that once White recaptures...} 11...b5 {We can go for the quick ...b5. We've managed to push our pawns first and want to create a pawn storm on the queenside, with the rook joining on c8. Also, the bishop not controlling the b5-square anymore made this even faster.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Yugoslav Attack / Study 1: White Plays 8.Bc4  / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/4107b8a9-7ef0-4a39-aad3-49e557373521/studies/cdcac7d9-a15d-47cb-b5b8-15cf917f724b/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 1 showed the main line, which is sharp but our setups somewhat depended on White's attacking choice. It's time to take a look at the slower 8.Bc4 in Chapter 2. It is designed for White to take control of the center early but takes time and if White is not careful, we could be the one with a deadly attack before they know it!} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight is out on f3. And we'll connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Expanding in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. We'll develop and attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White protects the center. Let's go for the Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be3 {White gets the dark-squared bishop out. And we'll fianchetto. We would like to play ...Ng4.} 6...Bg7 7.f3 {But White stops it with f3. Let's castle instead. Chapter 1 looked at 8.Qd2, preparing castling.} 7...O-O 8.Bc4 {However, we'll cover 8.Bc4 which you will face 15% of the time. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop first to have it be part of the attack and defense from the b3-square. But, as I mentioned, this move allows us to develop and organize our pieces for an attack with an extra tempo. And we should make use of that, so let's develop the knight first.} 8...Nc6 9.Bb3 {White could spend another tempo bringing the bishop back to b3. Sure, they've established it as an additional piece participating in the game, but gave us two free moves to maneuver. We'll activate the bishop, as per usual. The rook is coming to c8.} 9...Bd7 10.O-O {White castling short doesn't actually change our plans. Even though we can't attack the king on the kingside, our active moves still improve our position substantially.} 10...Rc8 {We'll bring the rook to the active c-file anyway and look to rotate the knight to either c4 or capture the bishop on b3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 6 Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/051fc536-f2fe-4ed1-91e3-4e1098673918/studies/47361bec-0ecf-4e63-86b1-3e668a05489e/lines"]

1.e4 {So far, we only looked at the 6.Be3 setups for White. In particular, those where White aimed to trade off our dark-squared bishop on h6 with the queen on d2. But what if White doesn't play like this and chooses to place the dark-squared bishop elsewhere or even activate the light-squared bishop first? Chapter 3 will deal with this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Preparing d4. We expect it.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. And we'll develop and pressure the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. We'll choose the Dragon, as we did so far. Our kingside setup is simple: we'll fianchetto on g7 and castle kingside. Our knight is coming to c6.} 5...g6 6.Bg5 {Study 1 will show the lines after 6.Bg5, and this move is played over 15% of the time in more than 1.1 million games. White pressures our knight but their ideas actually stay similar, with the queen coming to d2 and the bishop coming to h6 at some point. Since White's plan stays the same, so will ours. Developing the bishop and protecting the knight is natural.} 6...Bg7 7.Qd2 {White lines up the queen with the bishop. And we'll castle. White has a choice here.} 7...O-O 8.Bh6 {They could opt for the quick trade on h6. Going for Bg5 and avoiding losing a tempo with f3 allows this, especially when we don't have a knight on c6 yet. We'll develop now. There are no tricks, but the trade on g7 is fine.} 8...Nc6 9.Bxg7 {White captures. We'll take back with the king. White has achieved the trade they wanted, but there's no attack in sight. They would have to spend a lot of moves to build it up now.} 9...Kxg7 10.O-O-O {White castles long instead. Let's place the queen on b6 and pressure the knight on d4. White allowing the trades on d4 would equalize for us immediately and we wouldn't have anything to worry about.} 10...Qb6 11.Nb3 {If they still hope for some advantage, they will retreat to b3 and keep more pieces in the game. This gives us a chance to grab the momentum, however. By pushing the a-pawn and threatening to place it on a4.} 11...a5 12.a4 {Which is why White blocks our advancement. That move destabilizes the knight on b3, though.} 12...Be6 {Which we can attack with the bishop. I like the plan of 13...Rac8. You can even contemplate allowing 13.Nd4 Rac8 14.Nxe6+ fxe6 and we would have two open files for our rooks!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 6 Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/051fc536-f2fe-4ed1-91e3-4e1098673918/studies/47361bec-0ecf-4e63-86b1-3e668a05489e/lines"]

1.e4 {So far, we only looked at the 6.Be3 setups for White. In particular, those where White aimed to trade off our dark-squared bishop on h6 with the queen on d2. But what if White doesn't play like this and chooses to place the dark-squared bishop elsewhere or even activate the light-squared bishop first? Chapter 3 will deal with this.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Preparing d4. We expect it.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White advances in the center. Let's capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. And we'll develop and pressure the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. We'll choose the Dragon, as we did so far. Our kingside setup is simple: we'll fianchetto on g7 and castle kingside. Our knight is coming to c6.} 5...g6 6.Bg5 {Study 1 will show the lines after 6.Bg5, and this move is played over 15% of the time in more than 1.1 million games. White pressures our knight but their ideas actually stay similar, with the queen coming to d2 and the bishop coming to h6 at some point. Since White's plan stays the same, so will ours. Developing the bishop and protecting the knight is natural.} 6...Bg7 7.Qd2 {White lines up the queen with the bishop. And we'll castle. White has a choice here.} 7...O-O 8.O-O-O {If they castle, they become vulnerable to some tricks. For example, when we play ...Nc6, it's risky to go for Bh6 because we would have ...Nxe4 with a discovered attack on the knight.} 8...Nc6 9.Nxc6 {White doesn't want these complications, so they capture on c6 instead. We'll simply recapture. We close the c-file, but open up the b-file and the b2-pawn will become our main target. We will play against it by sliding the rook to b8 and bringing the queen to the queenside.} 9...bxc6 10.Bh6 {White goes for the trade now. Nothing we can do about that, but we have our own plans. We can even capture here.} 10...Bxh6 11.Qxh6 {White's queen coming to h6 isn't dangerous as there aren't any pieces to support it. Meanwhile, our attack could become dangerous quickly.} 11...Qb6 12.f3 {White goes for the slow buildup. But I reckon our expansion is quicker. The bishop is attacking the a2-pawn which is also weak, although defended.} 12...Be6 13.h4 {White starts the attack now, moving the h-pawn first. But they will have to deal with our pressure on b2 immediately.} 13...Rfb8 14.b3 {Pushing b3 is the most natural, but that weakens the knight on c3. Spotting these differences in the position can be crucial in the Sicilian. Like the b-file opening up, or the pawn being pushed to b3 and creating another weakness. We will attack the knight.} 14...Qb4 15.Kb2 {White defends. But now the b3-pawn is a target again, because it's pinned.} 15...a5 {So we will go for the obvious ...a5, which is one of the reason we played ...Rfb8 and not ...Rab8. We need both rooks on the queenside to attack properly. If we can, we will push the pawn to a4. If White plays a3, we can keep the queen on the b-file. The b3-pawn will be even weaker.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 6 Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 and 6.Be2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/051fc536-f2fe-4ed1-91e3-4e1098673918/studies/3720122a-23db-4730-8519-e247625ff44b/lines"]

1.e4 {Instead of playing 6.Bg5, White can also develop the light-squared bishop first. This usually signals that White wants a less confrontational game with a positional approach and often short castling. However, it is not always so.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight. Let's connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. And we'll attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be2 {A move that looks rather passive is Be2, being chosen 8% of the time. However, it is more flexible than Bc4 because White leaves their options open for transposing back to the main line. The bishop is safe on e2 and we don't know if White wants to castle short or play aggressively. We will develop anyway, no question about that.} 6...Bg7 7.Be3 {White goes for Be3 now. They could still short castle or play Qd2. We'll castle and await their next move. In fact, both options are viable.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castling short allows us to develop and put our pieces on the optimal squares, but provides no immediate chance for attack. Which is fine. We can get a good position in the Sicilian without any risks, too.} 8...Bd7 9.Qd2 {White lines up the queen with the bishop now. Perhaps they still want to trade the dark-squared bishops. But that's not going to be possible after ...Nc6, unless White trades on c6.} 9...Nc6 10.Rad1 {In any case, what they probably want to do is bring all the pieces to the center and focus their game there. When we don't expect Bh6 or anything of the sort, what we can expect is a move like f4. This goes well with the placement of White's pieces and grabs central space. We'll still get the rook to the c-file.} 10...Rc8 11.f4 {White expands with f4. They do block their dark-squared bishop for the moment, but get the necessary space to control the center and maybe even start a same side attack at some point! However, the e4-pawn is more vulnerable now. We'll trade on d4.} 11...Nxd4 12.Bxd4 {Our plan is that once White recaptures... We improve the bishop and place it on c6. This attacks the e4-pawn twice and doesn't give White time for an attack.} 12...Bc6 13.Bf3 {They have to defend the pawn instead. And it's time for us to expand.} 13...b5 {Which we can do with ...b5, now that there is no bishop controlling that square anymore! We don't even have to prepare it with ...a6. Our plan is simple: we want to attack the defender of the pawn on e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 6 Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 and 6.Be2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/051fc536-f2fe-4ed1-91e3-4e1098673918/studies/3720122a-23db-4730-8519-e247625ff44b/lines"]

1.e4 {Instead of playing 6.Bg5, White can also develop the light-squared bishop first. This usually signals that White wants a less confrontational game with a positional approach and often short castling. However, it is not always so.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight. Let's connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. And we'll attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Be2 {A move that looks rather passive is Be2, being chosen 8% of the time. However, it is more flexible than Bc4 because White leaves their options open for transposing back to the main line. The bishop is safe on e2 and we don't know if White wants to castle short or play aggressively. We will develop anyway, no question about that.} 6...Bg7 7.Be3 {White goes for Be3 now. They could still short castle or play Qd2. We'll castle and await their next move. In fact, both options are viable.} 7...O-O 8.Qd2 {As you know, White can go back to playing similarly to the main line, leading to a sharp battle. The main difference is that there is no pawn on f3, but rather a bishop on e2 guarding the g4-square!} 8...Nc6 9.O-O-O {We will use this immediately after White castles. And jump to g4, attacking the dark-squared bishop.} 9...Ng4 10.Bxg4 {This basically forces a trade, because White can't afford to give that bishop away. We'll capture, and now the rook is under attack. Our bishop on g7 has the full scope as well.} 10...Bxg4 11.f3 {White blocks with f3 now. But we'll return to e6, eyeing the queenside, and look to bring the queen and the rook next. This is also a provocative move because it allows Nxe6 which doubles our pawns.} 11...Be6 12.Nxe6 {White takes. We'll recapture with the pawn. This pawn structure is not a problem as White does neither have targets in the center nor on the kingside.} 12...fxe6 13.Bh6 {They can go for the trade...} 13...Qa5 {But we'll bring our queen to the queenside. The rook is coming to c8 to support it. If we have time, we can push the queenside pawns and attack down the line, even if the bishops get traded.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 6 Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 and 6.Be2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/051fc536-f2fe-4ed1-91e3-4e1098673918/studies/3720122a-23db-4730-8519-e247625ff44b/lines"]

1.e4 {Instead of playing 6.Bg5, White can also develop the light-squared bishop first. This usually signals that White wants a less confrontational game with a positional approach and often short castling. However, it is not always so.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {Developing the knight. Let's connect the pawns.} 2...d6 3.d4 {White expands in the center. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. And we'll attack the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White defends. The Dragon.} 5...g6 6.Bc4 {The bishop is developed to c4 13% of the time, in just over 950,000 games. White takes some control of the center and wants to castle short. Consider our plans nearly the same, with one important difference: the bishop on c4 allows for a quick queenside expansion with ...a6 and ...b5. We'll develop first.} 6...Bg7 7.O-O {White castles.} 7...O-O {And so will we. As I mentioned, our developing ideas stay the same, such as ...Bd7 and ...Nc6. However, look for that ...a6 and ...b5 strike when you can, because it comes with tempo and grabs queenside space we need anyway.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 1: White Plays 3.d4 and 3.Bc4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/fa808f7c-95bd-4b77-b47c-57dbe5fc7b53/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 4 will focus on White abstaining from the standard moves as early as move 3. This could be done for several reasons; maybe White doesn't know enough main line theory or thinks they can get an edge if they lead the positions into unknown territory. However, the main lines are the main lines for a reason and some early deviations could be considered suboptimal.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll create the pawn chain. Study 1 will cover 3.d4 and White recapturing with the queen, as well as 3.Bc4.} 2...d6 3.Bc4 {Developing the bishop to c4 on move 3 is chosen 18% of the time, in almost 18 million games! Instead of going into the open lines with d4, White wants to activate the bishop before playing d3. This leads to both bishops being activated to their respective squares and a big battle for space. We'll develop the knight, naturally.} 3...Nf6 4.d3 {White doesn't have to defend the pawn with the knight in this case. They create a pawn chain on the light squares, mirroring ours on the dark squares. The bishop is probably coming to g5. We'll just activate both knights.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, having developed the kingside. And we'll go for the standard idea. An idea we could go for at some point is ...Rb8 and ...b5. The bishop on c4 is often a target in the Sicilian.} 5...g6 6.Bg5 {Since White hasn't played d4, there is no point for bishop to develop to e3 or f4. So it comes out to g5 now to attack the knight. But it's conveniently defended by our bishop.} 6...Bg7 7.Nc3 {White continues natural development, but we won't just let them develop without problems. Let's make the bishop a target. The position is pretty closed.} 7...h6 8.Bh4 {So if the bishop retreats to h4... We can chase it with ...g5.} 8...g5 9.Bg3 {It has to go back to g3. Let's develop and exploit even more targets. When we develop rapidly in this setup, it might catch White off guard that they have to defend when they've expected us to castle a long time ago.} 9...Bg4 10.h3 {They can attack our bishop once...} 10...Bh5 {But once it retreats to h5, the obvious threat is ...Nd4. I'd say we've posed a lot of problems to White even in a peaceful line like this. We'll castle at some point soon, of course.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 1: White Plays 3.d4 and 3.Bc4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/fa808f7c-95bd-4b77-b47c-57dbe5fc7b53/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 4 will focus on White abstaining from the standard moves as early as move 3. This could be done for several reasons; maybe White doesn't know enough main line theory or thinks they can get an edge if they lead the positions into unknown territory. However, the main lines are the main lines for a reason and some early deviations could be considered suboptimal.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {White activates the knight. We'll create the pawn chain. Study 1 will cover 3.d4 and White recapturing with the queen, as well as 3.Bc4.} 2...d6 3.d4 {Going for d4 here is not a deviation by itself. But we haven't considered what happens if White recaptures with the queen, which is certainly possible.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {It's only played 6% of the time, though, and in almost 3 million games. White brings the queen to the center early and intends to keep it there. The queen will become a target immediately, which is the obvious downside of this move. When you attack the queen like this...} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {Expect the bishop to pin your knight on c6. This was White's plan all along. That's fine. We will unpin and force White to either capture on c6 or retreat with the queen.} 5...Bd7 6.Bxc6 {They capture. I suggest we actually recapture with the pawn here. We want to reach a specific pawn structure that will have a lot of central potential.} 6...bxc6 7.Nc3 {What I mean is that once White develops the knight... We can fight for the center with ...e5. This push takes space and comes with tempo.} 7...e5 8.Qd3 {White retreats to d3, keeping their queen somewhat active...} 8...Be7 {And we can finally start to develop the kingside. Our bishop comes out to e7 to continue guarding the pawn on d6. Our knight will develop to f6, of course, and we'll castle. We went for this pawn structure because it's extremely solid and allows for quick development. Also, the b-file is open which could come in handy at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 2: The Alapin and Others / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/a812bed1-c43b-4329-8892-ab22e2182fc7/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study will show further examples of early deviations by White, specifically on move 3. This includes 3.c3, 3.Nc3 and 3.Bb5+. Our setups will certainly have to change depending on what White plays, so let's see how our Dragon approach works here.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll strengthen the pawns.} 2...d6 3.Nc3 {When you see White going for a setup with the two knights, this is what you should think about. Nc3 on move 3 is played 8% of the time. They haven't used the chance to play d4, so why shouldn't we control it with additional pieces when we can? That's when developing the knight to c6 first becomes logical.} 3...Nc6 4.Bb5 {White pins our knight, but we will insist.} 4...e5 {We will take full control of d4! Now White will probably have to resort to playing d3 and keeping their position much more passive than it could have been. We'll develop the kingside next.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 2: The Alapin and Others / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/a812bed1-c43b-4329-8892-ab22e2182fc7/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study will show further examples of early deviations by White, specifically on move 3. This includes 3.c3, 3.Nc3 and 3.Bb5+. Our setups will certainly have to change depending on what White plays, so let's see how our Dragon approach works here.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll strengthen the pawns.} 2...d6 3.Bb5+ {3.Bb5+ is played only 6% of the time, and it gives us a healthy position and a lot of central space in the long run. There is no advantage in White trading the light-squared bishops early. We'll block with the bishop.} 3...Bd7 4.Bxd7+ {No point in White retreating now. We'll recapture with the queen.} 4...Qxd7 5.O-O {One idea could be that White wanted to play with tempo and castle, so they got the kingside pieces out as quickly as possible. That's fine. Since White hasn't played d4 yet, we'll develop the queenside knight first and reinforce our control of that square. There's another idea we have in the center.} 5...Nc6 6.c3 {White prepares d4 now, but we are quicker. We can actually push ...d5 here! White has done nothing to prevent it, and we've got the opportunity to expand in the center before they do. This idea is enhanced by White playing c3.} 6...d5 7.exd5 {Because when they take in the center... We can recapture with the queen and there is no Nc3 to attack it.} 7...Qxd5 8.d4 {White goes for d4 instead. But we don't have to capture now and allow Nc3 to come in the future.} 8...e6 {We can play ...e6 instead to open up our bishop. The other two kingside moves are standard. We've got a lot of central presence and White hasn't achieved anything playing like this.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 2: The Alapin and Others / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/a812bed1-c43b-4329-8892-ab22e2182fc7/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study will show further examples of early deviations by White, specifically on move 3. This includes 3.c3, 3.Nc3 and 3.Bb5+. Our setups will certainly have to change depending on what White plays, so let's see how our Dragon approach works here.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll strengthen the pawns.} 2...d6 3.Bb5+ {3.Bb5+ is played only 6% of the time, and it gives us a healthy position and a lot of central space in the long run. There is no advantage in White trading the light-squared bishops early. We'll block with the bishop.} 3...Bd7 4.Bxd7+ {No point in White retreating now. We'll recapture with the queen.} 4...Qxd7 5.c4 {5.c4 resembles the Maróczy Bind pawn structure with the pawns on e4 and c4. White is dominating the d5-square but leaves the d4-square unattended. Which is great for our dark-squared bishop as no pawns will block it in the center.} 5...g6 6.Nc3 {The knight comes out... And we'll fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.d4 {White could be ambitious and try to place all three pawns in the center. But we won't let them keep that for long.} 7...cxd4 8.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures. And we'll continue developing. The developing moves on the kingside remain unchanged almost no matter what White plays.} 8...Nf6 9.f3 {Before they develop the bishop to e3, White wants to control the g4-square. We'll castle. The knight is coming out next.} 9...O-O 10.Be3 {White develops the bishop. But we create more tension in the center.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O {Finally, White castles. And here's how to approach this specific position where White has the pawns on e4 and c4.} 11...e6 {We'll play a small center structure first, with the pawns on e6 and d6. The rooks belong to the center as that is where the battle will eventually be. White's main idea behind the pawns on the light squares was to control the d5-square, but that is exactly where we want to push! Trading on d5 will give life to our position and enable the ...b5 push in the future.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 2: The Alapin and Others / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/a812bed1-c43b-4329-8892-ab22e2182fc7/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study will show further examples of early deviations by White, specifically on move 3. This includes 3.c3, 3.Nc3 and 3.Bb5+. Our setups will certainly have to change depending on what White plays, so let's see how our Dragon approach works here.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll strengthen the pawns.} 2...d6 3.Bb5+ {3.Bb5+ is played only 6% of the time, and it gives us a healthy position and a lot of central space in the long run. There is no advantage in White trading the light-squared bishops early. We'll block with the bishop.} 3...Bd7 4.Bxd7+ {No point in White retreating now. We'll recapture with the queen.} 4...Qxd7 5.d4 {White going for d4 now basically changes nothing, except the light-squared bishops have been traded. We'll just capture.} 5...cxd4 6.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures, and you know the drill. We'll activate the kingside knight to f6, attacking the pawn on e4.} 6...Nf6 7.Nc3 {White defends...} 7...g6 {And we'll prepare the fianchetto and castling next. Our queen being on d7 and the bishops being absent makes no difference whatsoever.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 2: The Alapin and Others / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/a812bed1-c43b-4329-8892-ab22e2182fc7/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study will show further examples of early deviations by White, specifically on move 3. This includes 3.c3, 3.Nc3 and 3.Bb5+. Our setups will certainly have to change depending on what White plays, so let's see how our Dragon approach works here.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll strengthen the pawns.} 2...d6 3.c3 {White going for c3 on move 2 or 3 is called the Alapin Sicilian, and you should face it around 10% of the time. It's been played in more than 8.5 million games. White wants d4, but the e4-pawn is now weaker as the c3-square is occupied by the pawn. Still, we need to be careful in this position. We aren't actually attacking the pawn because if we were to capture it, White would have the fork on a4.} 3...Nf6 4.Be2 {They develop the light-squared bishop instead. Let's go for a Dragon-like approach, with ...g6. You know where the bishop is going.} 4...g6 5.O-O {White castles. And we'll fianchetto, also ready to castle.} 5...Bg7 6.Re1 {White centralizes the rook. They want to defend the e4-pawn with it and then play d4. But we won't make it that easy for them. Let's get the king out of the way first.} 6...O-O 7.Bf1 {White opens up the rook to go ahead with their plan. Sure enough, the e4-pawn is defended and they are aiming for d4. How to stop it? We will play ...e5! Even though we block our own bishop, we need to take as much control of d4 as possible.} 7...e5 8.d4 {They can still play d4, but it's different now. We'll take with the c-pawn first, although it makes no difference.} 8...cxd4 9.cxd4 {Because we will capture with both pawns anyway. Now White won't be able to keep a pawn on d4.} 9...exd4 10.Nxd4 {They get the knight to it instead.} 10...Nc6 {But we'll develop and offer a trade. Our main weakness in the position is the d6-pawn, which we will solve by pushing ...d5 very soon and trying to trade. Our bishop is in the clear now and probably our strongest piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Early Deviations by White / Study 2: The Alapin and Others / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/37d7eb2d-cbed-4dc0-9581-b289ef2d688a/chapters/81049466-a5a8-43b3-9b9e-4626c598b506/studies/a812bed1-c43b-4329-8892-ab22e2182fc7/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study will show further examples of early deviations by White, specifically on move 3. This includes 3.c3, 3.Nc3 and 3.Bb5+. Our setups will certainly have to change depending on what White plays, so let's see how our Dragon approach works here.} 1...c5 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll strengthen the pawns.} 2...d6 3.c3 {White going for c3 on move 2 or 3 is called the Alapin Sicilian, and you should face it around 10% of the time. It's been played in more than 8.5 million games. White wants d4, but the e4-pawn is now weaker as the c3-square is occupied by the pawn. Still, we need to be careful in this position. We aren't actually attacking the pawn because if we were to capture it, White would have the fork on a4.} 3...Nf6 4.d3 {White could switch their plans and go for d3 here. The pawn structure looks passive and unnecessary but is solid. Although White has given up on any opportunity to be the offensive side.} 4...g6 {We'll simply play ...g6, Dragon style. What's crucial about this variation is that we want to play ...e5 and completely lock down the d4-square, disallowing White from ever playing it if they haven't already. When we lock down the center, we will feel much more comfortable about advancing on a side of the board, like with ...b5 and ...b4.} *